ll_weight": 0.13, "fundamentals": {"margin": 3.17, "lean": -3.0, "environment": 8.3, "incumbency": -2.0, "money": -0.13}, "rating_margin": -0.25, "sigma_race": 6.3, "sigma_total": 6.99, "baseline_margin": 0.91, "analyst_adjustment": -0.41, "key_factors": ["Ten raters converge on Tossup, with only slight Republican tilts among the outliers", "Prediction markets price Huizenga around 52-53 percent, near even", "Thin, sponsor-skewed polling: the latest Democratic-sponsored survey gives McCann a one-point lead", "Huizenga's cash advantage is offset by $1.1 million in outside spending against him"], "rationale": "Every major rating outlet and both active prediction markets place this race at even or a hair Republican, while the only recent poll, Democratic-sponsored, shows McCann ahead by one. Huizenga's fundraising edge is largely neutralized by heavy outside opposition spending, leaving a genuine tossup with a faint Democratic tilt.", "watch": "A late independent poll or a decisive break on the gas-price issue could push the race clearly toward either candidate.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Incumbent Bill Huizenga (R) is running."]}, "overview": "Michigan's 4th District sits at the center of the House battlefield. Ten ratings outlets largely agree: seven call the race a Tossup, with Inside Elections at Tilt R, Decision Desk HQ at Lean R, and FPO alone at Lean D. Prediction markets price Huizenga's chances in the low fifties. Polling is sparse and sponsor-skewed — a Democratic-sponsored September survey put McCann up one, while Republican-sponsored spring polling had Huizenga ahead by six — leaving no clear measured edge.\n\nMoney roughly balances in effect: Huizenga has raised more and holds a cash advantage, but outside groups have spent over $1.1 million attacking him while McCann draws little outside help. The campaign is engaged, with Buttigieg stumping for McCann and energy prices weighing on the incumbent. The race lands at an even margin with a faint Dem